  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>, current through inductor is zero,</p><p>hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>eq</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#8214;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>18</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>E</mi><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>E</mi></mrow><mn>18</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>&#8734;</mo><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;inductor becomes a simple wire and now the circuit will be as shown in figure</p><p>hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>eq</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#8741;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#8741;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>33</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>;</mo></math>&nbsp;(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8741;</mo><mo>&#8658;</mo></math> parallel)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>E</mi><mrow><mn>33</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>E</mi></mrow><mn>33</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/9a019e65-ed37-4366-8f83-aa8f20cf21b2-image.png\" style=\"width: 220px; height: 164px;\" /></p>",
